WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.936
Now to a CNN exclusive.

00:00:02.936 --> 00:00:05.005
The Senate GOP leader, Mitch McConnell,

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:06.639
he says that he's not confident

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:07.807
Republicans will take back

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:09.442
the Senate majority in 2024.

00:00:09.442 --> 00:00:11.177
Leader McConnell telling CNN's moderates

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:12.312
who in an exclusive candid

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:14.280
phone interview, quote You'll love this.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.615
We do have the possibility

00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:17.017
of screwing this up

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:17.817
and that gets back

00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:19.185
to candidate recruitment.

00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:20.253
I think that we lost Georgia,

00:00:20.253 --> 00:00:21.488
Arizona and New Hampshire

00:00:21.488 --> 00:00:22.355
because we didn't

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:23.723
have competitive candidates

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:24.858
that would beat last year.

00:00:24.858 --> 00:00:25.592
He means

00:00:25.592 --> 00:00:27.761
this despite an obvious advantage

00:00:27.761 --> 00:00:29.329
in the upcoming election.

00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:31.097
Democrats are defending a whopping

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:33.700
23 seats, compared to only 11

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.468
Republican held seats.

00:00:35.468 --> 00:00:35.802
Right here

00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:36.903
in studio with me as our chief

00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:38.972
congressional correspondent, Manu Raju.

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:40.807
His candor is borne of experience.

00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:42.342
He thought in the last couple of cycles

00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:43.009
he would get

00:00:43.009 --> 00:00:45.045
and then pad a Republican majority.

00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:47.280
And yet he is in the minority.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.214
Let's just put the map up

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:48.915
on the screen here.

00:00:48.915 --> 00:00:50.316
If you look at this, number one,

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:51.151
the advantage for

00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:51.785
the Republicans

00:00:51.785 --> 00:00:53.286
is all 11 of those red states

00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:54.821
with Republican incumbents

00:00:54.821 --> 00:00:55.922
are Republican states

00:00:55.922 --> 00:00:57.023
and presidential elections.

00:00:57.023 --> 00:00:57.624
Is Florida

00:00:57.624 --> 00:00:59.626
potentially a swing state maybe.

00:00:59.626 --> 00:01:01.127
But you go through all of those states

00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:02.462
and you have Republican incumbents in

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:04.831
what should be safe or safe ish seats.

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:05.932
You look at the map here.

00:01:05.932 --> 00:01:06.933
McConnell in the interview

00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:07.200
with you

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.401
focuses on Montana

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:10.370
West Virginia, Ohio and Pennsylvania,

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:11.704
where you have Democratic incumbents

00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:14.107
right now in what, in a presidential year

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:15.241
should be tough states, right?

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:15.508
Yeah.

00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:17.410
And it's interesting to see him

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:18.144
talk about that.

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:19.112
Because he's seeing this

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:20.280
despite all those states

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.181
that we just saw.

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:21.714
He's really

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:23.583
looking at a very small map

00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:24.150
to take back

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.718
the majority in West Virginia.

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:26.553
Of course, Joe Manchin,

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:28.154
we don't know if he's going to run again.

00:01:28.154 --> 00:01:29.923
Be very happy about getting Jim

00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:31.958
just as the governor there to run there.

00:01:31.958 --> 00:01:33.726
Also in Ohio,

00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:35.328
he says Sherrod

00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:36.362
Brown is the incumbent

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:37.097
Democratic senator.

00:01:37.097 --> 00:01:38.231
They have several candidates

00:01:38.231 --> 00:01:40.300
they are happy about in Montana.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.568
There's hope that they could get

00:01:41.568 --> 00:01:42.135
this businessman

00:01:42.135 --> 00:01:43.803
named Tim Sheehy to run there.

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:45.538
Jon Tester is the incumbent member.

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:46.406
And they are really,

00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:47.974
really pining to get Dave

00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:49.843
McCormick, who ran and fell short

00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:51.244
in the Pennsylvania Senate race

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:52.512
in the last primary.

00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:54.180
Now they want him to run here.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.515
But, John, in each of those states,

00:01:55.515 --> 00:01:57.417
there are very complicated

00:01:57.417 --> 00:01:59.052
primaries that could emerge

00:01:59.052 --> 00:02:00.386
and could complicate their efforts

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:01.321
to get back the majority.

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:01.788
So McConnell

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:02.789
made very clear

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:03.990
that unlike the last cycle

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.024
where the Republican leaders,

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:06.159
for the most part,

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:07.861
stepped away, from those primaries,

00:02:07.861 --> 00:02:09.729
they pledged to engage, spend money

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:10.530
and resources

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:11.498
in those races

00:02:11.498 --> 00:02:12.899
to ensure that they believe

00:02:12.899 --> 00:02:14.601
they can get the most electable candidate

00:02:14.601 --> 00:02:15.702
route out the weaker ones.

00:02:15.702 --> 00:02:16.769
But of course, that's easier

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:17.337
said than done.

00:02:17.337 --> 00:02:18.638
And so let's continue there.

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:19.873
So another reason

00:02:19.873 --> 00:02:21.374
McConnell thinks Montana, West

00:02:21.374 --> 00:02:22.709
Virginia, Ohio, Pennsylvania,

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:23.643
good for Republicans,

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:25.278
even if Trump is the nominee,

00:02:25.278 --> 00:02:26.579
because those are strong states

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:26.980
for Trump.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:28.081
He lost Pennsylvania last time,

00:02:28.081 --> 00:02:29.349
but he has got a ton of votes.

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:30.617
It was a very close state there.

00:02:30.617 --> 00:02:31.651
Then you look at these other ones.

00:02:31.651 --> 00:02:32.552
You look at Wisconsin,

00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:33.253
you look at Nevada,

00:02:33.253 --> 00:02:34.154
you look at Arizona,

00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:35.522
where you have Democratic incumbents in

00:02:35.522 --> 00:02:37.457
what should be a battleground states.

00:02:37.457 --> 00:02:39.159
Why not more confident there?

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:39.726
Yeah, he knew.

00:02:39.726 --> 00:02:41.294
I asked him specifically about this.

00:02:41.294 --> 00:02:42.529
What about those other states?

00:02:42.529 --> 00:02:43.830
He left those out.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:44.797
Probably because the down

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:45.498
ticket, in fact,

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:46.332
he didn't say that

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:47.667
explicitly that the down

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:48.201
ticket, in fact,

00:02:48.201 --> 00:02:49.068
could potentially hurt

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:50.303
Wisconsin, could help

00:02:50.303 --> 00:02:52.539
but hurt them in Arizona.

00:02:52.539 --> 00:02:53.139
Michigan,

00:02:53.139 --> 00:02:55.175
he barely even mentioned in the interview

00:02:55.175 --> 00:02:55.875
in Arizona.

00:02:55.875 --> 00:02:58.244
Is such a complicated state for them

00:02:58.244 --> 00:03:00.613
because of the potential of

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:01.648
Kirsten cynical running

00:03:01.648 --> 00:03:03.016
as a third party candidate,

00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:04.450
also the Democratic candidate

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.419
and some very far right candidates,

00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:08.421
including Kerry Lake, Blake Masters,

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:10.590
considering a run in Arizona.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:11.824
McCollum made clear to me, John,

00:03:11.824 --> 00:03:12.992
that they planned to wait

00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:13.826
almost certainly

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:15.595
until after the primary to decide

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:16.162
whether to spend

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:16.729
any money

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:17.931
there at all,

00:03:17.931 --> 00:03:18.831
which explains

00:03:18.831 --> 00:03:20.400
the underlying problem here for them,

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:21.234
how the primaries could

00:03:21.234 --> 00:03:22.001
produce candidates

00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:23.136
who could be unelectable

00:03:23.136 --> 00:03:24.037
in a general election,

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:25.572
which is why he's hedging his bets

00:03:25.572 --> 00:03:26.139
at this point.

00:03:26.139 --> 00:03:27.106
One of the things he said to you is

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:28.441
we don't have an ideological litmus

00:03:28.441 --> 00:03:29.342
test will be involved

00:03:29.342 --> 00:03:30.176
in every general election

00:03:30.176 --> 00:03:30.944
where we have a legitimate

00:03:30.944 --> 00:03:32.245
shot of winning.

00:03:32.245 --> 00:03:33.980
The question is in the primaries, though,

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:35.882
does he expect to have fights with Trump

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:36.983
of a Republican nominee?

00:03:36.983 --> 00:03:37.650
Are they going to spend

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:38.351
to go against Trump?

00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:39.619
It's really an interesting question.

00:03:39.619 --> 00:03:40.587
What will Trump do?

00:03:40.587 --> 00:03:41.654
We don't really have a sense

00:03:41.654 --> 00:03:42.755
if he'll be as aggressive

00:03:42.755 --> 00:03:43.456
as he was last time

00:03:43.456 --> 00:03:44.624
because a lot of this blew up

00:03:44.624 --> 00:03:46.192
back in his face in the last cycle.

00:03:46.192 --> 00:03:47.794
What will he do now?

00:03:47.794 --> 00:03:49.395
Steve Daines, the NRC chairman,

00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:50.763
the Senate GOP campaign

00:03:50.763 --> 00:03:51.431
has been working

00:03:51.431 --> 00:03:52.532
behind the scenes, talking

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:53.933
to Trump, endorsed Trump

00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:54.934
for his presidential run.

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:56.002
Perhaps that could help things

00:03:56.002 --> 00:03:58.271
going forward, but it's still uncertain.

00:03:58.271 --> 00:03:59.339
Fascinating interview.

00:03:59.339 --> 00:04:00.607
And interesting

00:04:00.607 --> 00:04:03.509
is this again, his lack of confidence

00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:05.078
born of history.

00:04:05.078 --> 00:04:05.912
Manu, everybody,

00:04:05.912 --> 00:04:07.447
I urge you to go to CNN Politics.

00:04:07.447 --> 00:04:08.715
I can't read the entire interview.

00:04:08.715 --> 00:04:09.415
It's fascinating as.

